+# Design: slugify maxLength option
+
+## Approach
+
+Build the full slug exactly as today. If `options` is `undefined`, return it.
+Validate `options` (AC7) and `options.maxLength` (AC6) before any work. If
+`maxLength` is `undefined` or the full slug fits, return it. Otherwise take
+`slug.slice(0, maxLength + 1)`: if the character at index `maxLength` of the
+full slug is `-` (or the slug ends there), the first `maxLength` characters
+end at a word end; otherwise cut back to the last `-` within the first
+`maxLength` characters. If there is no such `-` (the first word is too long),
+return `slug.slice(0, maxLength)` (AC4). The result never ends in `-`
+because a cut is only made immediately before a separator.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- Split into words and accumulate while the joined length fits: clearer but
+  allocates an array per call; rejected only on taste, either is acceptable.
+- Regex `^.{0,n}(?=-|$)`: compact but hard to read and easy to get off by one;
+  rejected.
+- Throw `RangeError` for out-of-range integers: more idiomatic, but the
+  constitution (6) says `TypeError`; rejected.
+
+## Interface and data changes
+
+`slugify(input: string, options?: { maxLength?: number }): string`. Additive.
+The one behavioral change for existing calls: a second argument of `null`, a
+number or a string, previously ignored, now throws (AC7). The user accepted
+this in spec-clarify.
+
+## Risks
+
+- Off-by-one at the exact boundary (a slug of length `maxLength`, a cut that
+  lands exactly on a separator). Covered by AC2/AC3 tests.
+- Callers passing `null` as options (see above).
+
+## Rollback plan
+
+Revert the implementation commit(s); the option is additive, so no stored
+data or caller depends on it until they opt in.
